{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Patchset 2 — `Verified -1` triaged: 2 of the 3 failing jobs were MY defect\n\n### The two `build-and-test` failures were one defect, and it was mine\n\n```\ncomment-hygiene: 8 rot-prone reference(s) found\n  tests/unit/test_gen_env_registry.py:33   [commit-sha]  269b4d5678, d361eb28e4\n  tests/unit/test_gen_env_registry.py:225  [commit-sha]  269b4d5678, 93695b3721\n  tests/unit/test_gen_env_registry.py:266  [commit-sha]  269b4d5678, 4b524b5a41,\n                                                         74517a190e, d361eb28e4\n```\n\nAll 8 are in comment blocks **I wrote**. `scripts/check_comment_hygiene.py` (policy\n`b047-267f-3c3d-4374`) forbids bare commit SHAs in comments and docstrings — *\"commit SHAs\nrot (Gerrit's rebase-on-submit guarantees the pre-land SHA dies at merge); the ticket system\nowns historical detail\"* — unless the same block cites a durable reference.\n\nBoth red `build-and-test` jobs are the **same** finding reached two ways:\n- `lint + type-check` (1m10s) — the `comment-hygiene gate` step,\n  `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml:157`.\n- `pytest` (15m09s) — `tests/unit/test_comment_hygiene_guard.py::test_the_real_tree_is_clean`,\n  which shells out to the same script. Full result: **`1 failed, 15169 passed, 66 skipped,\n  1 xfailed`** — that single failure was the whole pytest job, so nothing else in this change\n  regressed anything.\n\n**Why my local evidence missed it, precisely.** Two independent reasons, and both are on me:\n1. `comment-hygiene` is a **CI-only step**. It is *not* in `make lint` — unlike the drift gate,\n   which `Makefile:221` deliberately mirrors locally \"so the local verdict agrees with CI\".\n   My `make lint` exit 0 was therefore correct and could never have caught this.\n2. The pytest half **would** have caught it: `tests/unit/test_comment_hygiene_guard.py` is an\n   ordinary unit test. I never got `tests/unit` to complete locally (host at load 22-24 from\n   other sessions), and this is exactly the gap that let it through. Reported as a caveat last\n   time; it turned out to be the live one. Worse: I introduced the line-266 SHAs in a docstring\n   tense-correction made *after* my last `make lint`, and re-ran only the single test file.\n\n**Fix:** every SHA replaced with the durable reference the policy asks for — the RP-04 cutover\nstories `cookable-governing-cockatoo` (1b07), `complex-leftist-drafthorse` (d074),\n`toadyish-magic-arrowana` (2a95), `cultish-shadowy-hapuku` (ba8b), plus\n`defiable-distortive-pinniped` for the ff2e fix and `84c7-486f` for this bug. The comments say\nthe same things and now point at records that outlive a rebase.\n\n### The third failure was NOT mine\n`optionality / clean core wheel` (37s) is change 1996's missing if-present guard: the workflow\ndefinition comes from `main` (the job log confirms\n`_build-and-test.yml@refs/heads/main (02f71da0976e)`) while the tree is the patchset's, so a\nstep added in the same commit as the file it runs hits `collected 0 items` / exit 4 on any\nchange based before it. Tracked as `anxious-resistant-urson`. Rebasing onto current `main`\nresolves it — verified locally: the file is now present and\n`pytest tests/interfaces/contracts/test_cli_registry_distribution.py` gives **4 passed**\ninstead of exit 4. No `recheck` used on any job.\n\n### Also folded in — the two LLM-Review advisories from patchset 1\n- `scripts/gen_env_registry.py:153` named `_stale_helper_rows`; the function is\n  `_raise_for_stale_rows`. Dangling name corrected — a real one, and exactly the class of drift\n  this change exists to stop.\n- The oracle's `_defined_function_names` caught only `SyntaxError` while the generator's census\n  catches `(SyntaxError, OSError)`. Now matched, with a comment recording why this does not\n  compromise the oracle's independence: independence is about not REUSING the generator's\n  function, not about disagreeing on which files are readable — an unreadable file must drop\n  out of both sides or the oracle would report a stale row the generator never raises on.\n\n### Verification on patchset 2 (rebased onto `02f71da097`)\n| Gate | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `scripts/check_comment_hygiene.py` | **exit 0** (was 8 findings) |\n| `tests/unit/test_comment_hygiene_guard.py` | **21 passed** (was the sole pytest failure) |\n| `make lint` | exit 0, ratchet `active=49 new=0 increased=0 stale=0` |\n| `make typecheck` | Success, 618 files |\n| held-out oracle + affected surface + both failing files + the optionality file (10 files) | **154 passed** |\n| `tests/interfaces/contracts/test_cli_registry_distribution.py` | **4 passed** (was exit 4) |\n\n**Full local sweep still not run** — the host is now at load ~54 from other sessions. I am not\ngoing to claim otherwise; CI's dedicated runner is the oracle, and its patchset-1 run already\ntold me the suite is otherwise clean at 15169 passed. Patchset 2 is CI run 32488951833.\n\nChange 1999 patchset 2, commit `a9303f0cb8`, Change-Id unchanged\n(`I0145c504f0aadbec480c2caf993fed1ebb8f9d1e`). Ticket stays `in_progress` pending\n`LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1`."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787320288158348001,"uuid":"626523f3-078b-4827-aceb-adc6b906855c"}